Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 18:49:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Photos that load, the cost to own first, acreage on every
 card, a lined-up compare

Photos: the MLS photo server allows 240 photos a minute for every visitor together, and each
is a full-size file of about 750 KB. The "All photos" gallery gave every photo its own width,
zero until it loaded, so all 33 counted as in view and were asked for at once: past the limit,
and the captions showed squeezed in a strip. Every photo now has a fixed box, the gallery is a
grid that loads as the reader scrolls, the map's list draws 24 homes at a time, a photo that
fails shows a calm panel and is asked for once more a minute later, and pages open the photo
server's connection early.

Listing page, signed up: under the price, what the home costs to own and how the listing is
moving (HOA fee and what it covers, property tax, flood zone, days on market and any price
cut, the next open house); then the description and every photo; then the rest. Photos and
detailed information are what buyers rate most useful (NAR 2025 Profile of Home Buyers and
Sellers: 81 and 77 percent).

Cards show the lot in acres. The map's 2D and 3D buttons sit outside the map Google redraws,
and 3D keeps the list following the view, the area search and the preview card.

Analyzer: comparing shows one table, the two scenarios in lined-up columns with the better
number marked; "About this home" is gone and "What we noticed" gives 5 to 7 one-line insights;
the repair check reads 4 photos, not 8.

